From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yewsifofficial <yewsifofficial@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 02:22:10 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] net: number online sockets from 3, as WindowSmash does

The traced device run: Smash's NEX client sends its WebSocket request on
socket 0, reads once (would block), then polls the socket six times, each
answering "readable" with the server's 101 waiting, and never reads it before
it closes the socket and reports 2306-0502. The likeliest reading: the client
takes descriptor 0 as "no socket" when it dispatches readiness (not yet proven). WindowSmash, whose sockets the same
client reads, numbers them from 3. Online sockets now start at 3; event
descriptors keep their numbers from 0.

Upstream-Status: fork-only (online play is out of nxnative's scope)
Reason: Nextendo online play for Super Smash Bros. Ultimate
Issue: TODO

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017xdHQoEipBX2wTqN8BNnMv
---
 dm/host/hle_net.c | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dm/host/hle_net.c b/dm/host/hle_net.c
index ccd9a20..5441060 100644
--- a/dm/host/hle_net.c
+++ b/dm/host/hle_net.c
@@ -213,7 +213,8 @@ int dm_sk_select(int nfds, uint64_t *r, uint64_t *w, uint64_t *e, const int64_t
 // in slices, giving up the guest core meanwhile (hle_thread.c), on sockets the host keeps non-blocking.
 enum { SK_AF_INET = 2, SK_SOCK_STREAM = 1, SK_SOCK_DGRAM = 2, SK_NONBLOCK_TYPE = 0x20000000, SK_O_NONBLOCK = 4, SK_O_NONBLOCK_ALT = 0x800,
        SK_F_GETFL = 3, SK_F_SETFL = 4, SK_SOL_SOCKET = 0xffff, SK_SO_SNDTIMEO = 0x1005, SK_SO_RCVTIMEO = 0x1006, SK_MSG_DONTWAIT = 0x80 };
-typedef struct { uint8_t len, family; uint16_t port; uint32_t addr; uint8_t zero[8]; } sk_in;   // nn::socket::SockAddrIn, network order
+typedef struct { uint8_t len, family; uint16_t port; uint32_t addr; uint8_t zero[8]; } sk_in;
+enum { SK_FD_FIRST = 3 };   // the first socket descriptor (see on_socket)   // nn::socket::SockAddrIn, network order
 static int on_err(int e) { t_skerr = e; return -1; }
 // DM_NEXTENDO_LOG: the game's calls on a bridge socket (the first ones of each kind), to see what it does with the server's answer
 static _Atomic int g_trace_n;
@@ -252,7 +253,10 @@ static int on_socket(int family, int type, int proto) {
     int one = 1; setsockopt(h, SOL_SOCKET, SO_NOSIGPIPE, &one, sizeof one);
     pthread_mutex_lock(&g_efd_m);
     int fd = -1;
-    for (int i = 0; i < EFD_MAX && fd < 0; i++) if (!g_efd[i].used) fd = i;
+    // Sockets are numbered from 3, never 0-2: Smash's NEX client wrote its request on socket 0, was told the answer was ready, and never read
+    // it (most likely it takes a descriptor of 0 as "no socket" when it dispatches readiness); WindowSmash numbers its sockets from 3 and the same client
+    // reads there. Event descriptors keep the numbers from 0 that offline games rely on
+    for (int i = SK_FD_FIRST; i < EFD_MAX && fd < 0; i++) if (!g_efd[i].used) fd = i;
     if (fd >= 0) {
         memset(&g_efd[fd], 0, sizeof g_efd[fd]);
         g_efd[fd].used = 1; g_efd[fd].host = h; g_efd[fd].pr = g_efd[fd].pw = -1; g_efd[fd].type = type; g_efd[fd].nonblock = nb;
